#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4020541d-b158-5694-b381-6ba67f8e0fa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{196 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558938} {:answer/id #uuid "4757fc24-3b66-50f2-9608-ded9a4e2810b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "272 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558939} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f6f4b4-0951-5767-8e49-9e2c75c41a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "353 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558954} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d2213f-eb4a-56a0-b993-71fb6d52fdd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{625 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558958} {:answer/id #uuid "c15608a7-e710-593f-9bfd-3bc890df6bb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 272 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4757fc24-3b66-50f2-9608-ded9a4e2810b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "272 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558939} :answer-field/id #uuid "596aed75-fbff-545e-94b0-567c1e8c389c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d0e8fd21-3500-5007-9a3c-aa39f399cf8f" :question/problem "![](images/b7/b73bdd66e1e9ac758c98d854bfe93b6fcf82ff8d85322f607e50b75a5797770f.png)\n\nGiven $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x^{2} + y^{2},\n$$ calculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy\n$$ over the positively oriented annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}P\\,\\mathrm dx+Q\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 5,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x^{2} + y^{2}\\,\\mathrm dA & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{5}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{5}\\\\ & =2 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(\\frac{625}{4}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cdot \\left((5)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\cdot \\left(625-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =272 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3049bbac-81f9-5220-9af1-9aa47b7cf658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 350 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558935} {:answer/id #uuid "a431815c-7a85-5b9c-8b60-bcccf96dc0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "350 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558955} {:answer/id #uuid "c50710fd-1e48-5ef7-a0b4-bd5e5698ad57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{296 \\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558964} {:answer/id #uuid "efcdabfc-a8f4-5979-8d11-663f01462fbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 674 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558969} {:answer/id #uuid "fac8ee63-1e41-5a4a-a535-7bac2932e547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 512 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558970}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3049bbac-81f9-5220-9af1-9aa47b7cf658" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 350 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558935} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a78c00f-da46-5e8a-8243-86c98db8401d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186558941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e873ebd8-54e2-5508-997d-3107e1ab89bf" :question/problem "![](images/c4/c4b3ac630020f1c4bd2d19dfb311b00fc0af41876497ce4772838e90ced60bd7.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle \\oint_{\\partial D}(x^{2} y + y^{3})\\,\\mathrm dx+(- x^{3} - x y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x^{2} y + y^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=- x^{3} - x y^{2}.$ So,\n\nFor $\\partial P/\\partial y$ we hold $x$ fixed, and for $\\partial Q/\\partial x$ we hold $y$ fixed. The component derivatives, including zero contributions, are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(x^{2} y + y^{3}\\right)=\\left(3 y^{2}\\right)+\\left(x^{2}\\right)=x^{2} + 3 y^{2}\\\\ \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} & =\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}\\left(- x^{3} - x y^{2}\\right)=\\left(- 3 x^{2}\\right)+\\left(- y^{2}\\right)=- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x^{2} + 3 y^{2},\\qquad\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}.$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_{\\partial D}(x^{2} y + y^{3})\\,\\mathrm dx+(- x^{3} - x y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dy & =\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D\\left(- 3 x^{2} - y^{2}-(x^{2} + 3 y^{2})\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D(- 4 x^{2} - 4 y^{2})\\,\\mathrm dA\\\\ & =\\iint_D - 4 \\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):3\\le r\\le 4,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}.$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D - 4 \\left(x^{2} + y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm dA & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}((r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}r^{2}(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)\\cdot r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\\\ & =-4\\cdot \\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\,\\mathrm d \\theta\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot \\int_{3}^{4}r^{3}\\,\\mathrm d r\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot \\frac{r^{4}}{4}\\big|_{3}^{4}\\\\ & =- 8 \\pi\\cdot (\\left(64\\right)-\\left(\\frac{81}{4}\\right))\\\\ & =-1\\cdot\\left(2 \\pi\\right)\\cdot \\left((4)^{4}-\\left((3)^{4}\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 2 \\pi\\cdot \\left(256-\\left(81\\right)\\right)\\\\ & =- 350 \\pi.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1745c0ec-91b5-533b-a488-e2bea21d90fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560061} {:answer/id #uuid "43db1007-ab9e-5837-ae42-58fe3ae09f69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560068} {:answer/id #uuid "83c9bb48-59e4-5ab1-b9c6-4e4c60698276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560086} {:answer/id #uuid "c384aa9d-923b-599e-bc01-7f1f75575e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560103} {:answer/id #uuid "e91c22bd-30c7-57db-9f87-d82ec928f0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1745c0ec-91b5-533b-a488-e2bea21d90fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560061} :answer-field/id #uuid "63c02e25-8ee6-5c9f-b462-f48435c790de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44f48d44-16c7-5f68-afd1-234e4ffcf06f" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=5$, $y=0$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=3$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(5x+1)\\,i+3z\\,j+xy\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5 x + 1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =5+0+0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}5\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}5\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}5\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[5x]_{x=0}^{x=5}\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (3-0) \\\\  & =(25-0)\\cdot 4\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =300.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560071 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5712fa33-2654-560a-953b-8ab341a87dac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560076} {:answer/id #uuid "be649ad5-b4e9-5d0c-a1a5-39aa52ff2977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560098} {:answer/id #uuid "c20f5bf6-6c83-5812-875c-16020afddb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560102} {:answer/id #uuid "d8ca3ef4-5b6a-5f8d-b1e9-486e15852860" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560108} {:answer/id #uuid "db3cd37d-26c8-565b-a403-fb51d1549af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{76}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c20f5bf6-6c83-5812-875c-16020afddb1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "114" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560102} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba1ad2f9-147b-5908-9366-77e6127caa05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "46a20a9b-cc15-5359-b9ee-c5e59873229e" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=2$, $x=3$, $y=1$, $y=2$, $z=0$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=2}^{x=3}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(27-8)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =114.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3324145e-7687-5d49-96a6-669054ec659f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "560" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560066} {:answer/id #uuid "47e66ac9-ee46-5c7a-8e67-1eb9e32c798a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560072} {:answer/id #uuid "5ccd7816-11a4-5bce-9d80-c8d67c51a34e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560080} {:answer/id #uuid "749a79f0-6e45-50da-938f-c51698f56e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560084} {:answer/id #uuid "ab9b93a4-f2e3-5271-a9ea-41397d389507" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "840" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "749a79f0-6e45-50da-938f-c51698f56e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1680" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560084} :answer-field/id #uuid "97a800ab-9ed7-5211-b33b-dbc03e4af6ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560089}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "57bd4c96-69f4-548a-a5d1-44f3f174d1f7" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=2$, $x=4$, $y=1$, $y=4$, $z=0$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{4}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=2}^{x=4}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=4}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(64-8)\\cdot (16-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =1680.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560078 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fbd81a5-13b8-5ffe-9128-1f49c13c0de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560059} {:answer/id #uuid "b371c1c0-9316-5576-9287-dda41e7e9011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-42" :db/id #edb/r