.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=2}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(8-1)\\cdot (4-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =42.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133fe0f6-3b54-5025-8f85-59b3f508b498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560060} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab8ebf9-29b9-57ad-9537-957e82e6e2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560064} {:answer/id #uuid "45e66bb7-f2af-5ce9-91d0-f2364b6fe2d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560070} {:answer/id #uuid "e1636939-8a89-5eda-ae40-2c56066069d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560111} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ec6b0a-dfa5-5db6-9637-5aed73fbe309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8ec6b0a-dfa5-5db6-9637-5aed73fbe309" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560115} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c27ed1e-c805-56df-b90b-eaa5a3199381" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2b380bb-5ecc-5211-8e29-58e3ad2580e7" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=0$, $x=4$, $y=0$, $y=3$, $z=0$, $z=2$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(3x+1)\\,i+3z\\,j+xy\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3 x + 1)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy) \\\\  & =3+0+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}3\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}3\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[3x]_{x=0}^{x=4}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =(12-0)\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =72.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c942fa1-574a-5714-9d2d-2c69b9d82fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560074} {:answer/id #uuid "b7c805b0-0b04-5961-a32c-3e6aac097806" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560095} {:answer/id #uuid "bec237d8-4f29-5dbe-bed4-f2b85d551520" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db