,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}2\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{5}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[2x]_{x=0}^{x=2}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (5-0) \\\\  & =(4-0)\\cdot 3\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560104 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d11677f-b41e-53c2-82d6-2e3f223790cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{416}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560067} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc3bf00-f3c4-575c-be10-687ff969f6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "416" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560083} {:answer/id #uuid "8d61fe98-57f2-508d-8d4a-f1a0ccc16cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{208}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560088} {:answer/id #uuid "db42b8fd-128f-574b-a418-bc6e345e7388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-416" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560110} {:answer/id #uuid "e308e49f-0fb4-5ca5-9e0e-32529b1c5871" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "208" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cc3bf00-f3c4-575c-be10-687ff969f6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "416" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560083} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecdb53e7-6fb3-56ce-9980-4f88e189d119" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c6fa7469-cadf-5bb3-8dec-3f881cb31af1" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1$, $x=3$, $y=1$, $y=3$, $z=2$, $z=4$. Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F={e}^{z}\\,i+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,j+4{x}^{2}yz\\,k$, find the flux of $F$ across $S$." :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(4{x}^{2}yz) \\\\  & =0+2 x^{2} y+4 x^{2} y \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}6{x}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}2y\\,\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=1}^{x=3}\\cdot [{y}^{2}]_{y=1}^{y=3}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & =(27-1)\\cdot (9-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =416.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186560117 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c318c6-6247-51e3-89f5-9e01e2e02c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{190}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560082} {:answer/id #uuid "c8b23a3e-7c17-526a-928a-85fad0743c35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-190" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560105} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd986c9-62eb-5305-bc07-9934063ce422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{76}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560118} {:answer/id #uuid "f659541f-1b8a-5155-9cf3-659c2f25168c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :d